  section#challenge
    h2 The challenge: speeding up transcription without sacrificing accuracy

    p Audra Hilse manages a long-running volunteer transcription project with a familiar pattern: many initial sign-ups, plus a dedicated core group that steadily moves work forward. Over time, the project shifted from primarily handwritten material to more typewritten pages—often with handwritten annotations.

    p Volunteers began asking a reasonable question: <em>Why start from a blank page when a draft might help?</em>

    p Audra experimented with generating drafts manually using Gemini inside Google Workspace, then moving that text into files for upload. The approach worked for a time, but it was fragile and difficult to sustain as a regular workflow.

    p When FromThePage released its Gemini 3 integration, Audra was able to test AI drafts directly inside the transcription interface—without needing to manage a separate, manual process.

  section#why-gemini
    h2 Why Gemini 3 drafts in FromThePage fit the project

    p Audra didn't want "automation" that replaced people. She wanted a workflow that respected her volunteers, supported scholarly standards, and made progress on a large and growing body of material.

    p Three FromThePage AI principles mattered immediately:

    h3 Optionality

    p Volunteers can choose whether to start from an AI draft or not. Audra reinforced this explicitly in her volunteer communications.

    blockquote "I emphasized in my newsletters… this is optional, you don't have to pull the AI draft in if you don't want to use it."

    p Some volunteers enjoy deciphering handwriting; others prefer reviewing a draft. Both approaches are valid—and supported.

    h3 Transparency

    p Audra values being able to say, clearly and honestly, when AI assisted in transcription.

    blockquote "I've appreciated how you guys have been talking about AI from the beginning… you want to keep it transparent."

    p FromThePage's direction—logging AI usage and labeling AI-assisted transcripts—aligned with her expectations for scholarly accountability and downstream reuse.

    h3 Tentativeness

    p Just as important: AI output should <em>feel</em> like a draft, not a final verdict.

    p Readable formatting, visible uncertainty, and easy editing help signal that the text still requires human judgment—especially in projects that prioritize authority and accuracy.

  section#practice
    h2 Putting it into practice

    p After testing Gemini 3 drafts on a small batch, Audra quickly adopted it as part of her regular workflow.

    blockquote "I'll just try this… on like three things and see how it works. And it worked fine, so all of my uploads since then, I've been using it."

    p From that point forward, AI drafts became the starting point—not the finish line.

  section#volunteer-adoption
    h2 Volunteer adoption: drafts that speed the work—without replacing review

    p Audra's volunteers have responded well to starting from an AI draft. Even when a draft needs light cleanup, it still saves time compared to retyping from scratch—and it keeps the work focused on what humans do best: checking meaning, resolving ambiguities, and ensuring accuracy.

    p That's exactly the role AI plays in FromThePage: a helpful starting point that remains clearly <em>tentative</em>, with people in control of the final transcript.

  section#human-review
    h2 Human review remains essential

    p For the Ayn Rand Archives, accuracy is not optional. Audra places her project closer to the "scholarly edition" end of the spectrum than to "good-enough-for-search" transcription.

    blockquote "There's a desire… to make sure that our transcripts are very accurate and authoritative. …some degree of human review is just probably always going to be part of our process."

    p AI drafts reduce the burden of first-pass transcription, but they don't remove the need for careful review. Volunteers still:

    ul
      li verify wording,
      li resolve handwritten edits,
      li correct formatting,
      li and flag unusual cases for follow-up.

    p This balance reassured Audra—and reinforced that volunteers still have meaningful, necessary work to do.

  section#publishing
    h2 Publishing for a community: read-only document sets

    p Audra's immediate goal isn't bulk export—it's access.

    p Before the organization's annual conference, she plans to publish batches of completed material using FromThePage's <strong>public, read-only document sets</strong>, allowing anyone to browse:

    ul
      li the original page image, and
      li the transcript displayed side-by-side.

    blockquote "We can tell people at the conference, 'you can just go read all of this now'… it's set up so nicely to show both the original page and then the transcript right next to it."

    p This approach lets the Archives share progress early, without waiting for a fully finalized corpus.

  section#looking-ahead
    h2 Looking ahead: preparing for scale

    p The Archives are planning for the future. Large portions of Ayn Rand's handwritten papers—potentially tens of thousands of pages—remain to be transcribed.

    p At that scale, starting from a blank page for every document would be overwhelming. Having AI drafts available as an <em>option</em> changes the equation: volunteers can review and refine instead of retyping every word, making long-term projects feel achievable while still preserving human oversight.

